.TH STYLE 9
.SH NAME
style \- choose what a relief looks like
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f5style\fP ?\f5classic\fP|\f5flat\fP?
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Style
sets, for the whole toplevel, what a
.B -relief
means when it is drawn.
With no argument it reports which is in force.
.TP
\f5classic\fP
Every relief is a two-tone chisel: a light edge and a dark edge, so that
.B raised
stands out of the surface and
.B sunken
is cut into it.
This is the default, and it is the look this toolkit has always had.
.TP
\f5flat\fP
Every relief is one restrained outline of the widget's border width, in
the darker of the two shades.
.B Raised
and
.B sunken
draw the same thing;
.B flat
still draws nothing.
.PP
It is a property of the toplevel rather than an option on each widget,
and deliberately so.
What makes a set of widgets look like one set is that they agree, and an
option each widget could set differently is an option each program would
set differently.
.PP
Collapsing
.B raised
and
.B sunken
loses no information a person could act on, because every state that
uses a relief to show itself also changes the fill behind it: a pressed
button is drawn in
.BR activebgnd ,
and a
.IR checkbutton (9)
or
.IR radiobutton (9)
with
.B -indicatoron
false is drawn in
.B select
when it is chosen.
The relief was reinforcing those, not carrying them.
.PP
Changing the style repaints every widget in the toplevel, since every
border is drawn from this.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
style flat
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR options (9),
.IR frame (9),
.IR button (9),
.IR wmtheme (2)
.SH BUGS
It changes borders only.
The colours, the padding and the corner shape are what else would have
to move for the two styles to look like two designs rather than one
design with its edges redrawn, and none of those is here.
.PP
A
.I frame
used purely as a divider, with a relief and no fill of its own, says
less in the flat style than in the classic one, because there the chisel
was the whole of what it drew.
.PP
There is no way to set it for a session, only per toplevel, so a program
that wants it must say so itself.
